=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- Distinguish overlap from independence
- Choose [addition]{color=blue} or [multiplication]{color=#9f5600}

=== layout: definitions

# Probability Conditions

@body

- OR: either event qualifies, including both.
- AND: both events must occur.
- Mutually exclusive: cannot happen together.
- Independent: knowing one happened does not change the other's probability.
- Given: use only outcomes consistent with what we know.

@notes

Mutually exclusive and independent describe different relationships. Keep the condition
attached to the rule rather than choosing a formula from a keyword alone.

=== layout: one-panel

# Context Before Calculation

@body

- Define the event and the population
- [OR: add; subtract shared outcomes]{color=blue}
- [AND: multiply; check independence]{color=#9f5600}
- Given: restrict to the known group
- Check totals against the biology

@notes

Use this sequence when deciding which probability rule applies. First name what we are
counting, then check the relationship between events.

=== layout: section

# Probability Rules

=== layout: subsection

# Addition Rule

## When Either Event Qualifies

=== layout: one-panel

# Addition Rules

@body

In general rules, *B and C* name two events. They are not allele names.

- [Specific Addition Rule]{color=blue}: if two events, B and C, are *mutually exclusive*, then:
  - [P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C)]{color=blue}
- [General Addition Rule]{color=blue}: if two events, B and C, are NOT mutually exclusive, then:
  - [P(B or C) = P(B) + P(C) &ndash; P(B and C)]{color=blue}

@notes

P means the probability of the event in parentheses. Read the condition before the formula.
OR includes both events; subtract the overlap because simple addition counts it twice.

=== layout: subsection

# Multiplication Rule

## When Both Events Must Occur

=== layout: one-panel

# Multiplication Rules

@body

- [Specific Multiplication Rule]{color=#9f5600}: if two events, B and C, are *independent*, then:
  - [P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C)]{color=#9f5600}
- [General Multiplication Rule]{color=#9f5600}:
  - [P(B and C) = P(B) &times; P(C|B)]{color=#9f5600}

P(C|B) is the probability that C occurs, given that B has occurred.

@notes

Read the vertical bar as "given." The general rule also works for independent events;
independence lets us replace P(C|B) with P(C).

=== layout: bookend

# Key Takeaways

@body

- [OR: add probabilities; subtract any overlap]{color=blue}
- [AND: multiply; account for dependence]{color=#9f5600}
- Read "given" as a restriction on possible outcomes

